/* Psalms: chapter summary + character study */
(window.CHAPINFO=window.CHAPINFO||{})[18]=[
[`The Psalter opens with two paths. The blessed person delights in the law of the Lord and meditates on it day and night, like a tree planted by streams of water, bearing fruit in season. The wicked are like chaff blown away, and the Lord watches over the way of the righteous.`,[['The righteous person','Rooted, steady and fruitful, whose joy is in God\'s word rather than in the crowd. Prosperity here means a life that is nourished and lasting.'],['The wicked','Restless and rootless, moving with every wind and having no lasting place in the assembly of the righteous.']]],
[`The nations rage against the Lord and His anointed, but God laughs from heaven. He installs His king on Zion, calls him His Son, and promises him the nations as his inheritance. The psalm ends with a call to kings to serve the Lord with fear and to take refuge in Him.`,[['The anointed king','Begins as David\'s own line and points to Christ. His authority comes from God\'s decree.'],['God','Unthreatened by human revolt, secure on His throne. He invites rebels to reconsider before judgment falls.']]],
[`A psalm of David when he fled from his son Absalom. Many say there is no help for him, but he declares that God is a shield around him and lifts his head. He lies down and sleeps, because the Lord sustains him. "From the LORD comes deliverance."`,[['David','Surrounded and betrayed by his own son, yet restful: he cries out, is answered, and sleeps in peace. Confidence in God is the antidote to fear.']]],
[`An evening prayer. David asks God to answer him when he calls, reminds those who love vain things to be still, and says that in peace he will lie down and sleep, because the Lord makes him dwell in safety. God has put more joy in his heart than when grain and wine abound.`,[['David','Calm and trusting at the end of the day. He challenges others not to sin in anger but to search their hearts on their beds and be silent.']]],
[`A morning prayer. David asks God to listen to his groaning and promises to wait in expectation. He knows that God does not delight in wickedness, and asks to be led in righteousness because of his enemies. All who take refuge in God can sing for joy.`,[['David','Starts the day by laying his requests before God and watching for the answer. His prayer is both humble and expectant.'],['God','Described as holy, hating evil but welcoming those who come to Him by His great mercy.']]],
[`David pleads that God not rebuke him in anger. Weak, with bones in agony and tears soaking his bed, he asks for mercy and healing. Suddenly he declares that the Lord has heard his weeping, and his enemies will turn back in shame.`,[['David','Weary and honest about being worn out. His turning point comes mid-psalm: the Lord has heard, and sorrow becomes confidence.']]],
[`David appeals to the Lord when pursued by an enemy, protesting his innocence and asking God to rise up in anger and judge the nations. He recognises that God is a righteous judge who is angry with the wicked every day, and the wicked fall into their own pit.`,[['David','Bold in asking for justice, willing to be examined by God. He trusts the Judge to set things right.'],['The wicked','Conceive trouble and give birth to disillusionment. Their violence returns on their own heads.']]],
[`"LORD, our Lord, how majestic is Your name in all the earth!" David looks at the heavens and asks, "What is mankind that You are mindful of them?" Yet God has made humans a little lower than the angels and crowned them with glory and honour, ruling over creation.`,[['David','Awed at both God\'s greatness and human dignity. He is humbled by the stars and amazed that God cares for people.'],['Humankind','Small but honoured, entrusted with care for animals, birds and fish.']]],
[`David thanks the Lord with all his heart, recalling how God upheld his right and rebuked the nations. He praises God as a stronghold for the oppressed and asks God to see his affliction. The nations are told they are only human.`,[['David','Grateful for specific deliverance. He remembers what God did and expresses thanks.'],['God','A refuge for the oppressed and one who does not ignore the cry of the afflicted.']]],
[`The psalmist asks why God stands far off in times of trouble. The wicked boast, hunt the weak and say there is no God. But the psalm ends with confidence that God hears the desire of the afflicted and defends the fatherless and the oppressed.`,[['The wicked man','Arrogant and cunning, convinced that God does not see. He lurks like a lion waiting for the helpless.'],['The helpless','Rely on God alone, and He lends an ear to their cry. The psalm shows that complaint and trust can co-exist.']]],
[`David says he takes refuge in the Lord. Friends advise him to flee like a bird, but he says that the Lord is in His holy temple and on His throne in heaven. God examines the righteous and hates the wicked, and the upright will see His face.`,[['David','Steady when advisers panic. He holds to his refuge: "If the foundations are destroyed, what can the righteous do?" and answers with the Lord\'s throne.'],['God','Sees everything, tests the righteous, and brings justice at last.']]],
[`David cries out because the faithful have vanished and everyone lies to their neighbour. God answers: "Because the poor are plundered and the needy groan, I will now arise." The words of the Lord are flawless, like silver purified seven times.`,[['David','Feels isolated in a deceitful society, but takes comfort in God\'s promise of protection.'],['God','Moves because of the groaning of the poor. His word is pure and reliable.']]],
[`"How long, LORD? Will You forget me forever?" David asks four times, feeling overwhelmed with sorrow. He asks God to give light to his eyes. Then he says, "I trust in Your unfailing love; my heart rejoices in Your salvation."`,[['David','Moves from lament to trust within six verses. He does not hide his pain, but he chooses to remember God\'s love.']]],
[`The fool says in his heart, "There is no God", and the corruption of humanity is described. God looks down to see if anyone understands, but all have turned aside. Yet God is with the company of the righteous, and the psalmist longs for salvation to come out of Zion.`,[['The fool','Not someone lacking intelligence but one who lives as if God does not matter. His practical atheism leads to corruption.'],['God','Surveys humanity and is a refuge to the poor.']]],
[`"Lord, who may dwell in Your sacred tent?" The answer: the one whose walk is blameless, who does what is righteous, speaks the truth, does no harm to others, keeps promises even at a cost, and does not lend at interest or accept bribes.`,[['The one who dwells with God','Honest in word and deed, loyal to promises, kind to neighbours and unbribable. The character of a person at home with God.']]],
[`David asks God to keep him safe, for in Him he takes refuge. He says, "Apart from You I have no good thing." He is thankful that his lines have fallen in pleasant places, and he says, "You will not abandon me to the realm of the dead... You make known to me the path of life."`,[['David','Content with God as his inheritance and cup. His delight in God\'s people and His counsel makes him say, "I keep my eyes always on the LORD".']]],
[`David cries for justice, asking God to hear a plea from lips free of deceit. He asks to be kept as the apple of God\'s eye and hidden in the shadow of His wings, and contrasts himself with the wicked, whose reward is in this life. "As for me, I will be vindicated and will see Your face."`,[['David','Confident in his integrity and aware of God\'s examination. He wants to be satisfied by seeing God\'s likeness when he awakes.'],['The wicked','Satisfied with treasure and children, but have no lasting hope.']]],
[`A long song of David after the Lord delivered him from his enemies and from Saul. "The LORD is my rock, my fortress and my deliverer." God thunders from heaven, parts the sea, and rescues him. David says that God rewarded him according to his righteousness, and "with my God I can scale a wall".`,[['David','Grateful, vivid and bold, telling the story of rescue in cosmic terms. He credits God for his strength, his skill and his victories.'],['God','Pictured as storm, shield and rock. He is faithful to the faithful, blameless to the blameless and humbles the proud.']]],
[`"The heavens declare the glory of God." Creation speaks without words, day after day, and the sun is a bridegroom coming out of his chamber. The law of the Lord is perfect, reviving the soul, making wise the simple. The psalmist ends by asking God to keep him from hidden faults and presumptuous sins.`,[['The psalmist','Moves from the book of nature to the book of Scripture and then to his own heart. He asks that his words and meditation be pleasing to God, "my Rock and my Redeemer".'],['God','Reveals Himself in the sky and in His word, both reliable and sweet as honey.']]],
[`A prayer for the king before battle. The people ask that the Lord answer him in distress, send help from the sanctuary, and grant his heart\'s desire. "Some trust in chariots and some in horses, but we trust in the name of the LORD our God."`,[['The king','Supported by the prayers of the people. His strength is not in his army but in God\'s answer.'],['The people','Pray for their leader and shout for joy at his victory, a model of intercession.']]],
[`The king rejoices in the Lord\'s strength, thanking God for answering his prayers and giving him a crown of pure gold and length of days. His confidence is that "through the unfailing love of the Most High he will not be shaken". The enemies will be consumed.`,[['The king','Glad in God\'s strength, trusting in love and not in himself. His joy is the result of what God has done.']]],
[`"My God, my God, why have You forsaken me?" The psalmist is surrounded by enemies, mocked, pierced in hands and feet, and his garments are divided. Yet the psalm turns to praise: "He has not despised or scorned the suffering of the afflicted one." Future generations will hear of His righteousness.`,[['The sufferer','Lives through abandonment and pain, but does not give up on God. The Gospels see Jesus on the cross in these words.'],['The mockers','Taunt that God should rescue him if He delights in him. Their jeers are echoed at the crucifixion.']]],
[`"The LORD is my shepherd, I lack nothing." He leads the psalmist beside quiet waters, restores his soul, guides him in right paths, and walks with him through the darkest valley. He prepares a table in the presence of enemies, and goodness and love will follow him all the days of his life.`,[['The shepherd','Provides, guides, protects and comforts. He is personal ("my shepherd") and present ("You are with me").'],['The psalmist','A sheep who learns to trust the shepherd\'s care in every season. His confidence is rooted in God\'s character, not in circumstance.']]],
[`"The earth is the LORD\'s, and everything in it." Who may ascend His holy hill? Those with clean hands and a pure heart. The gates are called to lift up their heads for the King of glory, the Lord strong and mighty in battle, the Lord Almighty.`,[['The one who ascends','Honest in heart and action, not living for false gods. Only God\'s grace makes this possible.'],['The King of glory','Entering the city in triumph. The picture is later applied to Christ\'s ascension.']]],
[`David lifts up his soul to the Lord, asking to be shown God\'s ways and taught His paths. He asks God to remember His mercies and not the sins of his youth. "The LORD confides in those who fear Him." He ends feeling lonely, afflicted, and asks God to redeem Israel.`,[['David','Humble, teachable and honest about his sin. He prays for guidance and for forgiveness.'],['God','Good and upright, instructing sinners in His ways and leading the humble.']]],
[`David asks the Lord to vindicate him, for he has walked in integrity. He asks God to examine his heart and mind. He avoids the company of the deceitful, washes his hands in innocence, and loves the house where God\'s glory dwells. "My feet stand on level ground."`,[['David','Confident in his integrity, invites God\'s testing. He worships with gratitude in the great assembly.'],['The wicked','Have blood on their hands and bribes in their right hands, a foil to David\'s clean conscience.']]],
[`"The LORD is my light and my salvation, whom shall I fear?" Even if an army besieges him, David will be confident. He asks one thing: to dwell in the house of the Lord and gaze on His beauty. Even if his father and mother forsake him, the Lord will receive him. "Wait for the LORD; be strong and take heart."`,[['David','Bold in the face of danger because his heart is fixed on God. He has one great desire, and he encourages himself to wait.']]],
[`David cries to the Lord, his Rock, asking not to be silent toward him or to be dragged away with the wicked. God hears his cry, and David praises: "The LORD is my strength and my shield; my heart trusts in Him, and He helps me." He prays for the people to be shepherded and carried.`,[['David','Desperate for an answer, and then quickly relieved. His prayer moves from the personal to the community.'],['God','A strength and shield, a saving refuge for His anointed and the shepherd of His people.']]],
[`"Ascribe to the LORD the glory due His name." The voice of the Lord is heard over the waters, breaking cedars, shaking the desert, twisting oaks and stripping forests bare. In His temple, all cry, "Glory!" The Lord sits enthroned over the flood and gives strength to His people and blesses them with peace.`,[['God','Powerful in the storm, His voice is mighty and majestic. Yet He gives strength and peace to His people.'],['The worshippers','Called to bow before His holiness in awe. The thunderstorm becomes a call to worship.']]],
[`A song for the dedication of the temple. David praises the Lord who lifted him out of the depths and healed him. "Weeping may stay for the night, but rejoicing comes in the morning." He cried for mercy and God turned his wailing into dancing, and he will give thanks forever.`,[['David','Grateful after being near death, remembering how prosperity once made him feel secure. God\'s favour is a gift, not a right.'],['God','Brings people up from the grave and shifts mourning into dancing, replacing sackcloth with joy.']]],
[`David takes refuge in the Lord and prays for rescue from his enemies and from shame. "Into your hands I commit my spirit." He admits that he is in distress, forgotten and surrounded by whispering, yet he says, "My times are in your hands." The psalm ends with a call to love the Lord and be strong.`,[[`David`,`Weak and afraid, yet he entrusts his life to God and ends with courage.`],[`God`,`A rock, a fortress and a storehouse of goodness for those who fear Him.`]],`Jesus quoted verse 5 on the cross, showing how to trust God in the darkest hour.`],
[`"Blessed is the one whose transgressions are forgiven." David describes how silence about his sin made his bones waste away, until he acknowledged it and God forgave. He urges the faithful to pray while God may be found and not to be like a stubborn horse or mule.`,[[`David`,`Honest after a season of hiding, then relieved and grateful at being forgiven.`],[`God`,`Forgives, teaches and counsels, and surrounds the trusting with unfailing love.`]],`Confession brings freedom, and forgiveness is a gift, not something earned.`],
[`The righteous are called to sing a new song to the Lord with skill and shouts of joy. His word is right and true, and by His word the heavens were made. No king is saved by a great army. The eyes of the Lord are on those who fear Him, and our hope is in His unfailing love.`,[[`The psalmist`,`Leads a congregation in joyful praise.`],[`The Lord`,`Creator by His word, and the one who frustrates the plans of nations.`]],`God's word created the world and still guides it, so He is worthy of trust and praise.`],
[`David, who once pretended to be insane before Abimelech to escape, praises the Lord at all times. He sought the Lord and was delivered from all his fears. "Taste and see that the Lord is good." The angel of the Lord encamps around those who fear Him, and the Lord is close to the brokenhearted.`,[[`David`,`Grateful and teaching others from his own experience of being rescued.`],[`The Lord`,`Hears the cry of the righteous and redeems His servants.`]],`God is near to the brokenhearted and invites people to find out for themselves that He is good.`],
[`David asks God to contend with those who contend with him and to fight against those who fight him. People who were once friends now plot and lie against him, though he had grieved for them in their illness. He waits for God to vindicate him and promises to praise Him among the great assembly.`,[[`David`,`Wronged and betrayed, he brings the case to God rather than taking revenge.`],[`His false accusers`,`Return evil for good and rejoice at his stumbling.`]],`When we are wronged, we can hand the case to the God who sees and judges justly.`],
[`The wicked person has no fear of God and flatters himself in his own eyes. By contrast, God's love reaches to the heavens, His faithfulness to the skies, and His righteousness is like the mighty mountains. People find refuge in the shadow of His wings, and with Him is the fountain of life.`,[[`The wicked`,`Plot evil even as they lie in bed, and have no fear of God.`],[`God`,`A source of unfailing love, an overflowing feast and the fountain of life.`]],`The contrast between human sin and God's love leads the psalmist to trust and to ask for protection.`],
[`"Do not fret because of evildoers." David says that the wicked will fade like grass, but the meek will inherit the land. He urges trust in the Lord, delight in Him, commitment of the way to Him, and patience. He recalls that he has never seen the righteous forsaken.`,[[`David`,`An older man who has watched life for many years and can say that God does not abandon His people.`],[`The righteous`,`Told to trust, be still and wait, because the Lord upholds them.`],[`The wicked`,`Seem to prosper, but their end is brief.`]],`In a world where evil often looks successful, the psalm calls God's people to patient, active trust.`],
[`In a prayer of deep distress, David feels God's rebuke and the weight of his guilt. His body is in pain and his friends keep their distance, while his enemies plot. He confesses his sin and waits for the Lord to answer.`,[[`David`,`Physically and spiritually broken, and honest about his sin.`],[`His friends`,`Stand far off, leaving him alone in his suffering.`]],`Even in the worst consequences of sin, a person can still cry to God and wait for His answer.`],
[`David resolves to guard his tongue, but his silence only stirs up his distress, and finally he speaks to God. He asks the Lord to show him how brief his life is, a mere handbreadth. Everyone is a breath. He prays, "My hope is in you," and asks to be spared before he is gone.`,[[`David`,`Reflects on life's brevity, and chooses honesty before God.`],[`God`,`The only lasting hope for people who are just passing through, as all our ancestors were.`]],`Remembering how short life is helps us to rely on God and number our days wisely.`],
[`David waited patiently for the Lord, who lifted him out of the slimy pit and put a new song in his mouth. Sacrifices and offerings were not what God wanted, but a heart that says, "Here I am, I have come to do your will." David asks for deliverance from present troubles.`,[[`David`,`Grateful for past rescue and still in need of help.`],[`The Lord`,`Hears, lifts up and gives a new song, and delights in obedience more than ritual.`]],`The psalm points forward to Christ's willing obedience, quoted in Hebrews 10.`],
[`Blessed are those who have regard for the weak, for the Lord delivers them in times of trouble. David is ill and his enemies speak against him, and even his close friend who ate his bread has turned against him. He prays for mercy and is sure that God upholds him. The psalm ends the first book of Psalms with praise.`,[[`David`,`Sick, slandered and betrayed, and yet he trusts God's care.`],[`The close friend`,`Has lifted his heel against David, a betrayal that Jesus later applied to Judas.`]],`God cares for those who care for the poor, and He stays faithful when friends do not.`],
[`As the deer pants for streams of water, so the psalmist's soul pants for God. He remembers leading worship at the house of God and asks why his soul is downcast. Tears have been his food, and enemies taunt him, "Where is your God?" He tells himself, "Put your hope in God."`,[[`The psalmist`,`A worshipper far from the temple, speaking honestly to his own soul.`],[`God`,`The living God whose presence satisfies deeper than anything else.`]],`Spiritual thirst is real, and the cure for a downcast soul is hope in God.`],
[`The psalmist asks God to vindicate him against an ungodly nation and to rescue him from deceitful people. He asks God to send out His light and truth to guide him to the holy mountain. He again tells his downcast soul to hope in God.`,[[`The psalmist`,`Feels forsaken but still calls God his stronghold and joy.`],[`God`,`Is asked to send light and truth, like guides, to bring His servant home.`]],`This psalm continues Psalm 42, and answers despair with a prayer for God's guidance.`],
[`The sons of Korah recall how God drove out the nations for their ancestors, and say they do not trust their own bow. But now God has rejected and humbled them, and they are scattered like sheep for slaughter, though they have not forgotten Him. "Awake, Lord! Why do you sleep?" they pray, appealing to God's unfailing love.`,[[`The sons of Korah`,`Faithful, yet suffering, and bold enough to question God.`],[`God`,`Is addressed as the one who once acted mightily and who must act again.`]],`It shows that faithful people can suffer without understanding why, and still bring their complaint to God.`],
[`A wedding song for the king, who is the most excellent of men and whose lips are anointed with grace. His throne endures for ever and ever, and he loves righteousness and hates wickedness. The bride is told to forget her people and honour her husband, and she is led to the king in joy.`,[[`The king`,`An ideal ruler who points forward to Christ, the final King.`],[`The bride`,`Arrives in beauty, and is called to belong fully to her husband.`]],`The psalm celebrates the royal marriage and looks ahead to the Messiah and His people.`],
[`"God is our refuge and strength, an ever-present help in trouble." Even if the earth gives way, the mountains fall and the waters roar, God's city will not fall. "Be still, and know that I am God." The Lord Almighty is with us, and the God of Jacob is our fortress.`,[[`God's people`,`Calm in the midst of chaos because God is with them.`],[`God`,`Ends wars, breaks bows, and will be exalted among the nations and in the earth.`]],`Fear gives way to quiet trust when we remember who God is and that He is with us.`],
[`All peoples are told to clap their hands and shout to God with cries of joy. The Lord Most High is awesome, the great king over all the earth, and God reigns over the nations. The leaders of the nations assemble as the people of the God of Abraham.`,[[`The nations`,`Invited, not just Israel, to praise the king over all.`],[`God`,`Enthroned as the king of all the earth, and our shields belong to Him.`]],`God's rule is universal, and His praise is meant for every nation.`],
[`Great is the Lord in the city of our God. Mount Zion, the city of the great King, is beautiful in its height. Kings who gathered against it fled in terror. The people ponder God's unfailing love in His temple and are told to walk around Zion and count its towers, and to tell the next generation that this God is our God for ever.`,[[`The people of Zion`,`Reflect on God's protection and pass the story on to their children.`],[`God`,`Makes His city secure and will be our guide even to the end.`]],`God's faithfulness to His people is something to remember and to retell.`],
[`The psalmist calls all people, rich and poor, to listen. No one can redeem the life of another or pay a ransom to God. The wealthy die like the foolish, leaving their wealth to others. But God will redeem the psalmist from the realm of the dead and take him to Himself.`,[[`The psalmist`,`A teacher of wisdom who is not afraid of the rich and powerful.`],[`The rich`,`Trust their wealth, but death comes to them as it does to animals.`]],`Wealth cannot save us from death, but God can.`],
[`God summons the whole earth and judges His people. He does not need their sacrifices, since every animal of the forest is His. He asks for thanksgiving, vows kept, and prayer in time of trouble. To the wicked who recite His laws but hate His instruction, He says, "I will rebuke you." Those who offer thanksgiving honour Him.`,[[`God`,`The judge who sees through empty religion and calls for sincere worship.`],[`The wicked`,`Use God's words but ignore His commands.`]],`God wants a thankful, obedient heart more than sacrifices.`],
[`After Nathan confronted David about Bathsheba, David prays for mercy. "Have mercy on me, O God, according to your unfailing love." He admits that his sin is always before him and that he has sinned against God. He asks to be washed, to have a clean heart and a renewed spirit, and says God desires a broken spirit.`,[[`David`,`Broken and honest, with no excuses, throwing himself on God's mercy.`],[`God`,`Forgives, cleanses and restores the joy of salvation.`]],`This is the classic prayer of repentance, and shows that God does not despise a broken and contrite heart.`],
[`David, writing after Doeg the Edomite told Saul where David had gone, asks why the mighty man boasts of evil. His tongue is like a sharpened razor. God will bring him down for ever. By contrast, David is like a green olive tree in the house of God, who trusts in God's unfailing love for ever and ever.`,[[`Doeg`,`Proud, deceitful, and loves evil more than good.`],[`David`,`Rooted in God's house, trusting and thankful.`]],`Those who trust in wealth or power will fall, but those who trust in God flourish.`],
[`A version of Psalm 14: fools say in their hearts, "There is no God." They are corrupt, and no one does good. God looks down from heaven to see if anyone understands, and finds that all have turned away. David prays for salvation to come out of Zion and for God to restore His people.`,[[`The fool`,`Lives as if God does not see or care.`],[`God`,`Searches humanity and finds universal need for rescue.`]],`Everyone falls short, and salvation must come from God.`],
[`When the Ziphites told Saul where David was hiding, David prays for God to save him by His name and vindicate him by His might. He says that God is his helper and sustainer. He promises a freewill offering and says God has delivered him from all his troubles.`,[[`David`,`Surrounded by betrayal, yet certain that God is his helper.`],[`The Ziphites`,`Local people who sell David out to Saul.`]],`God's name is a reliable refuge when people turn against us.`],
[`David is troubled by threats and by the betrayal of a close friend with whom he once walked into the house of God. He wishes he had the wings of a dove to fly away, but he calls on God, who hears him. "Cast your cares on the Lord and he will sustain you."`,[[`David`,`Anguished by a friend's betrayal and tempted to run away.`],[`The treacherous friend`,`Has smooth words, but his heart is war.`]],`We can hand our burdens to a God who will not let the righteous fall.`],
[`When the Philistines seized David in Gath, he prays for mercy because his enemies hound him all day long. "When I am afraid, I put my trust in you." He is confident that God has recorded his tears in His scroll, and what can mere mortals do to him?`,[[`David`,`Admits his fear, but moves to trust.`],[`God`,`Keeps count of sorrows and collects tears in His bottle.`]],`Fear and faith can exist in the same heart, and trust is the choice that wins.`],
[`David, hiding in a cave from Saul, cries out, "Have mercy on me, my God, for in you I take refuge. I will take refuge in the shadow of your wings." He is among lions, but he awakes the dawn with a song and asks that God be exalted above the heavens.`,[[`David`,`Cornered in a cave, yet filled with praise.`],[`God`,`A refuge, and He fulfils His purpose for David.`]],`Praise in the dark shows confidence that God's love and faithfulness are greater than our danger.`],
[`David asks rulers if they really judge uprightly. They plot injustice and are like snakes that stop their ears. He asks God to break their teeth and make them vanish. The righteous will be glad when they see vengeance, and people will say, "Surely there is a God who judges the earth."`,[[`The unjust rulers`,`Pervert justice and refuse to listen to correction.`],[`God`,`Is the final judge over all earthly judges.`]],`When human courts fail, there is a God who judges, and that brings hope to the oppressed.`],
[`When Saul sent men to watch David's house in order to kill him, David asks God to deliver him from enemies who prowl like dogs. He says God is his strength and his fortress, and that he will sing of His strength and love in the morning.`,[[`David`,`Watched and threatened, but sure of God's protection.`],[`God`,`Is the God of unfailing love who goes before him.`]],`God is a stronghold in the night and a reason to sing in the morning.`],
[`After a time of defeat, David says God has rejected and broken the nation, but asks Him to restore them. He recalls God's promise that Gilead, Manasseh and Ephraim are His. He asks for help against the enemy, because human help is worthless. "With God we will gain the victory."`,[[`David`,`Honest about a hard defeat and eager to trust God again.`],[`God`,`Has all lands as His own and gives victory in His time.`]],`When things go badly, the answer is not self-reliance but renewed dependence on God.`],
[`David cries out from the ends of the earth when his heart grows faint. "Lead me to the rock that is higher than I." He wants to dwell in God's tent for ever and take refuge in the shelter of His wings. He prays for the king and promises to fulfil his vows.`,[[`David`,`Far from home and weary, yet confident of God's protection.`],[`God`,`A refuge, strong tower and rock, higher than any difficulty.`]],`When we feel faint, God is a safe place to which we can go.`],
[`"Truly my soul finds rest in God; my salvation comes from him." David is attacked like a leaning wall, but he trusts God alone. He urges the people to pour out their hearts to Him, because those of low or high degree are but a breath, and power belongs to God.`,[[`David`,`Calm and sure, telling his own soul to rest.`],[`God`,`A rock, salvation, fortress and a God of power and love.`]],`Peace comes from placing all our hope in God alone.`],
[`David is in the wilderness of Judah, thirsty for God. He has seen God in the sanctuary and says His love is better than life. He will praise God as long as he lives, and his soul is satisfied as with a rich feast. He meditates on God through the night watches.`,[[`David`,`Longing for God as one longs for water in dry land.`],[`God`,`His love is better than life, and His right hand upholds the psalmist.`]],`Even in a desert, a person can find deep satisfaction in God.`],
[`David asks God to protect him from the conspiracy of the wicked, who sharpen their tongues like swords and shoot their arrows at the innocent. God will shoot them with His own arrows, and everyone will fear and proclaim what He has done.`,[[`David`,`Hears secret plans and lies and asks God for protection.`],[`The wicked`,`Confident they will not be seen, but God sees.`]],`Evil plots will not succeed against God's justice.`],
[`Praise awaits God in Zion, and He answers prayer. He forgives sin, brings His people near, and by awesome deeds answers with righteousness. God waters the earth, enriches it, and crowns the year with His bounty. The meadows and valleys shout for joy.`,[[`The people`,`Come to Zion with vows and are satisfied with the goodness of God's house.`],[`God`,`The hope of all the ends of the earth, who calms the roaring of the seas and gives the harvest.`]],`God is both the forgiver of sins and the provider of daily bread.`],
[`The whole earth is invited to shout for joy to God and to come and see what He has done. He turned the sea into dry land, and tested His people like silver, bringing them to a place of abundance. The psalmist comes to the temple with offerings and testifies that God has listened to his prayer.`,[[`The psalmist`,`Declares what God has done for him personally.`],[`God`,`Rules over the nations, and keeps His people's feet from slipping.`]],`Praise is meant to be shared, and God hears the prayers of those who keep their hearts clean.`],
[`A short prayer that God would be gracious to Israel and bless them, so that His ways may be known on earth and His salvation among all nations. May the peoples praise Him, and may the nations be glad and sing for joy, because He judges the peoples with equity.`,[[`The congregation`,`Asks for blessing so that the nations might also know God.`],[`God`,`Guides the nations and causes the land to yield its harvest.`]],`God blesses His people so that His salvation reaches the whole world.`],
[`"May God arise, may his enemies be scattered." This is a triumphant song celebrating God as the father to the fatherless and defender of widows. It recalls the exodus and Sinai, and God's ascent on high, leading captives in His train. Kingdoms of the earth are called to sing to God.`,[[`God`,`A warrior and a protector of the vulnerable, who daily bears our burdens.`],[`The kingdoms of the earth`,`Invited to sing praise to the God of Israel.`]],`The same God who is strong enough to defeat enemies cares for the lonely and weak.`],
[`David is overwhelmed, as if sinking in deep mire and floodwaters. He is hated without cause, and zeal for God's house consumes him. He is given vinegar for his thirst, a detail that the New Testament connects with Jesus. He asks God to answer in His abundant mercy, and ends in praise.`,[[`David`,`Exhausted from calling for help, but still hoping in God.`],[`His enemies`,`Hate him for no reason and mock his devotion.`]],`The psalm gives words to deep suffering and points to the sufferings of Christ.`],
[`A short prayer: "Hasten, O God, to save me." David asks that those who seek his life be put to shame, and that those who seek God rejoice and say, "Let God be exalted." He describes himself as poor and needy and asks that the Lord not delay.`,[[`David`,`Urgent and honest about his need.`],[`God`,`Helper and deliverer, who is not asked to wait.`]],`Prayer can be short and urgent, and God hears the cry of the needy.`],
[`An older person's prayer. The psalmist trusted God since birth, and now prays, "Do not cast me away when I am old." He proclaims God's righteous deeds, and says that he will keep on hoping and praising. God has taught him from his youth.`,[[`The psalmist`,`Aged, but passionately committed to telling the next generation about God.`],[`God`,`Has been his refuge from childhood and will not leave him in old age.`]],`God's faithfulness lasts through every stage of life.`],
[`A prayer for the king, that he would judge the people with righteousness and defend the afflicted. May he endure like the sun, and rule from sea to sea. He delivers the needy who cry out, and all nations will serve him. The book ends the second section of Psalms with a doxology.`,[[`The king`,`Rules with justice, mercy and compassion for the poor.`],[`God`,`Is blessed for ever, and does wonderful things.`]],`This is the picture of an ideal ruler, fulfilled in Jesus, the righteous King.`],
[`Asaph nearly slipped when he saw the prosperity of the wicked. They seem to have no struggles and mock God. He felt that his own purity was in vain, until he entered the sanctuary and understood their end. "Whom have I in heaven but you? ... God is the strength of my heart and my portion for ever."`,[[`Asaph`,`Honest about envy, then renewed by seeing things from God's perspective.`],[`The wicked`,`Appear to be at ease, but stand on slippery ground.`]],`The question of why the wicked prosper is answered by being near to God.`],
[`A lament about the destruction of the temple. Enemies have set the sanctuary on fire and mocked the name of God. Asaph asks God to remember His covenant and His congregation. God is called King from of old, who brings salvation, divided the sea and set the seasons.`,[[`Asaph`,`Bewildered at the sight of ruin, and asks God to act.`],[`God`,`Creator and King who has acted in the past and will not forget the cause of the poor.`]],`Even when worship places are destroyed, God remains King, and we can plead on His promises.`],
[`A song of thanks to God, whose name is near. God declares He will judge uprightly at the appointed time, when the earth quakes. He warns the arrogant not to lift up their horns. It is God who judges, bringing one down and exalting another, and the cup of wrath is in His hand.`,[[`God`,`The judge who chooses the time and decides who is lifted up.`],[`The arrogant`,`Are told to stop boasting.`]],`Promotion and power come from God, not from our own efforts.`],
[`God is known in Judah and His name is great in Israel. His dwelling is in Zion, where He breaks the weapons of war. Warriors are unable to lift their hands, and kings fear the God who is more majestic than the mountains full of prey. People should bring gifts to the God who is to be feared.`,[[`God`,`A glorious conqueror who stops the strongest armies.`],[`The warriors`,`Are powerless in the face of His rebuke.`]],`God's power is greater than any human strength, and He is to be revered.`],
[`Asaph cries aloud to God in the night, and refuses to be comforted. He asks whether God has forgotten to be merciful. Then he remembers the deeds of the Lord, His wonders of long ago, and meditates on how God led His people like a flock through the sea.`,[[`Asaph`,`Sleepless and sorrowful, then recovered by remembering what God has done.`],[`God`,`Whose path through the sea was unseen, yet He led His people by the hand of Moses and Aaron.`]],`Remembering God's past faithfulness is one way to find hope in present trouble.`],
[`A long history of Israel, told to teach the next generation. God gave His law and wanted each generation to trust Him, but Israel rebelled repeatedly, even after the plagues, the exodus and the manna. God was patient, rejected Shiloh and chose Judah and David as shepherd.`,[[`Asaph`,`Teaches parables from the past, warning the young not to be stubborn like their ancestors.`],[`Israel`,`Quick to forget God's works and slow to trust Him.`],[`God`,`Compassionate, forgiving and finally raising up David to shepherd His people.`]],`History is a warning and an encouragement, showing both human failure and God's patience.`],
[`Foreign nations have invaded God's inheritance, defiled the temple and left the dead unburied. Asaph asks, "How long, Lord?" and asks God to help for the glory of His name and forgive sins. He promises that God's people will praise Him for ever.`,[[`Asaph`,`Grieved at the nation's sorrow and aware of its sin.`],[`The nations`,`Mock God's people and shed their blood like water.`]],`In national disaster, the prayer is for forgiveness and for God's name to be honoured.`],
[`Asaph prays to the Shepherd of Israel, asking Him to restore His people and make His face shine on them. Israel is pictured as a vine brought out of Egypt which now lies in ruins, its walls broken down. He asks God to look down and care for this vine and to revive them.`,[[`God`,`Shepherd and keeper of the vine, whose face brings salvation.`],[`Israel`,`A vine once flourishing, now trampled and burnt.`]],`The refrain "Restore us" is a prayer for renewal that depends entirely on God.`],
[`A festival song calling Israel to sing for joy to God. God speaks: "I am the Lord your God, who brought you up out of Egypt." But His people would not listen. He says, "If my people would only listen to me ... I would feed you with the finest wheat."`,[[`Israel`,`Invited to celebrate, yet stubborn and unwilling to listen.`],[`God`,`Longs for His people's obedience in order to bless them.`]],`God's desire is to bless a people who will listen to Him.`],
[`God stands in the great assembly and judges among the gods. He asks how long they will defend the unjust and show partiality to the wicked. He tells them to defend the weak and the fatherless, and to rescue the poor. Because they have not, they will die like mere mortals. The psalmist asks God to rise and judge the earth.`,[[`God`,`The supreme judge who confronts the powers behind injustice.`],[`The rulers`,`Called gods, but will die like men.`]],`Justice for the weak is a divine priority, and all earthly rulers answer to God.`],
[`A coalition of nations plots to wipe out Israel's name. Asaph lists their names and asks God to deal with them as He did with Midian, Sisera and Jabin. He asks that they would seek God's name, so that they will know that He alone is the Most High over all the earth.`,[[`Asaph`,`Anxious about a united enemy and prays for God to intervene.`],[`The enemy nations`,`Conspire against God's own people.`]],`The aim of God's judgement is that the nations should know who He is.`],
[`"How lovely is your dwelling place, Lord Almighty!" The psalmist's soul yearns for the courts of the Lord. Even the sparrow has found a home near God's altar. Blessed are those who dwell in His house and whose strength is in Him. "Better is one day in your courts than a thousand elsewhere."`,[[`The pilgrims`,`Travel through the valley of Baca and make it a place of springs.`],[`God`,`A sun and shield who bestows favour and honour and withholds no good thing.`]],`Nearness to God is better than anything the world offers.`],
[`The sons of Korah thank God for restoring the land and forgiving His people's sin. They ask Him to restore them again and revive them. They listen for what God will say, which is peace for His faithful people. "Love and faithfulness meet together; righteousness and peace kiss each other."`,[[`The sons of Korah`,`Grateful for past mercy and hopeful for future revival.`],[`God`,`Speaks peace, and brings love and truth together.`]],`God's salvation joins justice and mercy, which finds its fullest meaning in Christ.`],
[`David prays, "Hear me, Lord, for I am poor and needy." He asks for mercy and joy, remembers that God is good, forgiving and abounding in love, and asks, "Teach me your way, Lord, that I may walk in your truth. Give me an undivided heart."`,[[`David`,`Humble, dependent and asking for a heart that fears God's name.`],[`God`,`Great, working wonders, compassionate and gracious.`]],`The best request is to know God's way and to have a united heart.`],
[`A short song about Zion, the city God founded. The Lord loves the gates of Zion more than all other dwellings. Rahab, Babylon, Philistia, Tyre and Cush are said to be among those who acknowledge God, and it is said of each, "This one was born in Zion."`,[[`Zion`,`The city of God, in which people of every nation will find their home.`],[`The nations`,`Are counted as born in the city of God.`]],`God's family includes people from every nation.`],
[`The darkest psalm, from Heman the Ezrahite. His soul is full of trouble and he is close to the grave. He feels that God has cast him into the depths and that friends have abandoned him. He cries to God every day, and the psalm ends with only darkness as his closest friend.`,[[`Heman`,`Suffers from long illness and loneliness, but keeps praying.`],[`God`,`The God of his salvation, to whom he still cries day and night.`]],`It shows that honest, unresolved lament is part of faith, and that God hears it.`],
[`Ethan the Ezrahite sings of the Lord's great love and faithfulness for ever. He recalls God's covenant with David and that his throne will last for ever. But now the king has been rejected and humiliated, and the writer asks, "How long, Lord?" The psalm ends with a call to praise.`,[[`Ethan`,`Praising God for His promises and puzzled at their apparent failure.`],[`God`,`Faithful to His covenant, even when His people see no sign.`]],`The promise to David finds its fulfilment in Jesus, even when circumstances seem to deny it.`],
[`A prayer of Moses, the man of God. "Lord, you have been our dwelling place throughout all generations." God is eternal, and human life is brief, like grass. Moses asks God, "Teach us to number our days, that we may gain a heart of wisdom," and for His favour to rest on their work.`,[[`Moses`,`Reflects on human frailty before the eternal God.`],[`God`,`From everlasting to everlasting, the home for each generation.`]],`Remembering that life is short leads to wisdom and a desire for God's favour.`],
[`"Whoever dwells in the shelter of the Most High will rest in the shadow of the Almighty." The psalm promises protection from the fowler's snare, the pestilence and terror by night. Angels will guard those who trust. God says, "Because he loves me, I will rescue him ... I will be with him in trouble."`,[[`The one who trusts`,`Finds that God is a refuge and fortress.`],[`God`,`Speaks in the last verses to promise rescue, honour and long life.`]],`Trust in God brings deep security, though Jesus showed it is not a licence to test God.`],
[`A song for the Sabbath day. It is good to praise the Lord and proclaim His love in the morning and faithfulness at night. The senseless do not understand, and the wicked flourish like grass only to be destroyed. The righteous flourish like palm trees and cedars, and still bear fruit in old age.`,[[`The psalmist`,`Rejoices in the works of God's hands.`],[`The righteous`,`Planted in God's house, and still fresh and green in old age.`]],`The Sabbath is a day to praise God and to remember His faithfulness.`],
[`"The Lord reigns, he is robed in majesty." The world is firmly established and cannot be moved. The seas have lifted up their voice, but the Lord on high is mightier than the thunder of the great waters. His statutes stand firm, and holiness adorns His house.`,[[`The Lord`,`King of all creation, whose throne is from of old.`],[`The seas`,`Roar, but are no match for the one who rules.`]],`God's rule is stable, so His people can be secure.`],
[`"Lord, the God who avenges, shine forth." The psalmist asks God to judge the proud, since the wicked are crushing God's people, and say that God does not see. The one who planted the ear can hear. Blessed is the person God disciplines. "When I said, 'My foot is slipping,' your unfailing love, Lord, supported me."`,[[`The psalmist`,`Troubled by injustice and comforted by God's care.`],[`The wicked`,`Kill widows and orphans, and say that God does not see.`]],`God hears and will do justice, and His discipline is a form of love.`],
[`"Come, let us sing for joy to the Lord." He is the great God and King above all gods, and the maker of the sea and the mountains. The people are called to kneel before Him, for He is our God. The psalm ends with a warning not to harden their hearts as at Meribah and Massah.`,[[`The worshippers`,`Invited to sing, bow down and listen.`],[`God`,`The Rock of our salvation, who also warns those who ignore His voice.`]],`Worship must be joined to listening, and hard hearts miss God's rest.`],
[`"Sing to the Lord a new song; sing to the Lord, all the earth." The psalmist calls the nations to declare God's glory, and says the Lord is to be feared above all gods because idols are nothing. All creation will sing before the Lord, who comes to judge the earth with righteousness.`,[[`The psalmist`,`Calls for a global mission of praise.`],[`The Lord`,`Great, to be praised, and coming to judge with justice.`]],`God's glory is meant to be told among all peoples.`],
[`The Lord reigns, so let the earth be glad. Clouds and thick darkness surround Him, and fire goes before Him. Those who worship idols are put to shame. Zion hears and rejoices. The psalm urges those who love the Lord to hate evil, because He guards the lives of His faithful ones.`,[[`The Lord`,`King over the earth, whose righteousness is the foundation of His throne.`],[`The righteous`,`Rejoice and give thanks to His holy name.`]],`God's rule means that evil will be dealt with, and the righteous can rejoice.`],
[`"Sing to the Lord a new song, for he has done marvellous things." His right hand and holy arm have worked salvation. The ends of the earth have seen His victory. The sea, rivers and mountains are called to join in praise, because the Lord comes to judge the world in righteousness.`,[[`The Lord`,`Has remembered His love and faithfulness to Israel and made His salvation known.`],[`The earth`,`Called to shout for joy.`]],`God's salvation is public, and all creation will celebrate it.`],
[`The Lord reigns, let the nations tremble. He is holy, and sits enthroned between the cherubim. The king loves justice, and God has established equity. Moses, Aaron and Samuel called on His name, and He answered them. God was forgiving to them, though He punished their wrongdoings.`,[[`The Lord`,`Holy, a lover of justice, forgiving yet not ignoring sin.`],[`Moses, Aaron and Samuel`,`Mediators who prayed and heard God answer.`]],`Holiness and mercy go together in the character of God.`],
[`"Shout for joy to the Lord, all the earth." Worship the Lord with gladness and come before Him with joyful songs. Know that the Lord is God, and we are His people, the sheep of His pasture. Enter His gates with thanksgiving, for the Lord is good and His love endures for ever.`,[[`The worshippers`,`Called to thank and praise God from all nations.`],[`The Lord`,`Maker, shepherd, and source of enduring love and faithfulness.`]],`A short and well-loved call to joyful and thankful worship.`],
[`David states the kind of king he intends to be. He will sing of love and justice, walk in his house with a blameless heart, and set no vile thing before his eyes. He will not tolerate slanderers or the arrogant, and will look for the faithful in the land to serve him.`,[[`David`,`Commits himself to personal integrity and to honest leadership.`],[`The faithful`,`Are those the king wants to dwell with him.`]],`Leaders are called to start with their own heart and household.`],
[`A prayer of someone who is afflicted and faint, pouring out a lament before the Lord. His days vanish like smoke, and he is like a lonely sparrow. Yet the Lord is enthroned for ever, and will rise and have compassion on Zion. The heavens will perish, but God remains the same, and His years will never end.`,[[`The afflicted one`,`Sleepless and lonely, but still looking to God.`],[`The Lord`,`Unchanging and eternal, listens to the destitute.`]],`When everything else passes away, God remains and will rebuild what is broken.`],
[`"Praise the Lord, my soul, and forget not all his benefits." He forgives sins, heals diseases, redeems life from the pit and satisfies with good things. The Lord is compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in love. "As far as the east is from the west, so far has he removed our transgressions from us." As a father has compassion on his children, so the Lord has compassion on those who fear Him.`,[[`David`,`Stirs his own soul to praise for all God has done.`],[`The Lord`,`A merciful Father who remembers that we are dust.`]],`One of the great psalms of God's forgiveness and tender love.`],
[`A hymn of creation. The Lord is wrapped in light, stretches out the heavens like a tent and sets the earth on its foundations. He gives water to the wild animals, grass for the cattle, and wine to gladden the heart. The sun, the moon and the creatures of the sea all depend on Him. "How many are your works, Lord! In wisdom you made them all."`,[[`The Lord`,`Creator and sustainer of all things, who renews the face of the earth.`],[`The creatures`,`Look to Him to give them their food in due season.`]],`Creation shows God's wisdom and ongoing care, and leads to praise.`],
[`A history psalm that recalls God's covenant with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, Joseph's rise in Egypt, the plagues and the exodus. God brought out His people with silver and gold, gave them the lands of the nations, and did it so that they would keep His precepts. "Praise the Lord."`,[[`God`,`Remembers His covenant for a thousand generations.`],[`Joseph`,`Sold as a slave, but raised by God to save the family.`]],`The history of Israel is a story of God's promise-keeping, to encourage obedience.`],
[`A history psalm that confesses Israel's repeated failures: at the Red Sea, in the wilderness, with the golden calf, at Baal Peor and at Meribah. They mixed with the nations and sacrificed their children to idols. Yet the Lord remembered His covenant and relented, and the psalmist asks God to save them again.`,[[`Israel`,`Quick to forget, but receives mercy again and again.`],[`Phinehas`,`Stood up and stopped the plague, and was counted as righteous.`]],`We join our ancestors in confessing sin and rely on God's persistent love.`],
[`"Give thanks to the Lord, for he is good; his love endures for ever." The redeemed are called to say so. Four groups cry to the Lord and are rescued: those lost in the desert, those in prison, the sick, and those caught in a storm at sea. He calms the storm, and turns deserts into pools.`,[[`The redeemed`,`Wanderers, prisoners, the sick and sailors who all cry out in their trouble.`],[`The Lord`,`Hears each cry and acts to deliver, and the wise will consider His love.`]],`God rescues people from every kind of trouble when they call on Him.`],
[`David's heart is steadfast, and he will sing and make music. He praises God's love, which is higher than the heavens, and asks for help against the enemies. The psalm combines parts of Psalms 57 and 60, and says, "With God we will gain the victory."`,[[`David`,`Resolute in praise and in looking to God for victory.`],[`God`,`Promises to take possession of the land and triumph over enemies.`]],`Praise and prayer for help are bound together, with confidence in God's victory.`],
[`David prays against a group of accusers who repay his love with hatred. His enemies speak lies, and he asks that a wicked person be appointed against them. He is poor and needy, and his heart is wounded. He asks God to deal with him for His name's sake and promises to praise the Lord in the great assembly.`,[[`David`,`Sorrowful and falsely accused, praying with strong language.`],[`The accusers`,`Return evil for good and hatred for love.`]],`It shows that raw anger can be brought to God, who stands at the right hand of the needy.`],
[`"The Lord says to my lord: 'Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet.'" The Messiah is promised to rule from Zion, and is declared a priest for ever, in the order of Melchizedek. He will judge among the nations and drink from the brook beside the way.`,[[`David`,`Speaks by the Spirit about one greater than himself.`],[`The Messiah`,`King and priest, referred to by Jesus and the writer of Hebrews.`]],`This psalm is the most quoted Old Testament passage in the New Testament, pointing to Christ's reign.`],
[`"Praise the Lord. I will extol the Lord with all my heart." The works of the Lord are great, and full of splendour and majesty. He provides food for those who fear Him, remembers His covenant, and shows His people the power of His works. "The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom."`,[[`The psalmist`,`Delights in studying God's works.`],[`The Lord`,`Gracious and compassionate, faithful and just in all He does.`]],`The psalm is an acrostic, and it ends by saying that knowing God is where wisdom begins.`],
[`"Blessed are those who fear the Lord." They delight in His commands, their children are mighty in the land, and wealth and riches are in their houses. They are generous, righteous and firm, with no fear of bad news. The wicked will see and be vexed, and their longings come to nothing.`,[[`The righteous person`,`Gracious, generous and secure, because his trust is in the Lord.`],[`The wicked`,`Gnash their teeth in envy and fade away.`]],`The psalm is a companion to Psalm 111, and shows that fear of God leads to generous living.`],
[`"Praise the Lord, you his servants." The Lord's name is to be praised from now and for evermore. He is exalted above the nations, and yet stoops to look on the heavens and the earth. He raises the poor from the dust and seats them with princes, and gives the barren woman a home.`,[[`The Lord`,`Great above all, and yet close to the poor and lowly.`],[`The poor and barren`,`Lifted up, as Hannah and Mary would celebrate later.`]],`God's greatness shows itself in His care for the humble.`],
[`When Israel came out of Egypt, the sea fled, the Jordan turned back, and the mountains skipped like rams. The psalmist asks why, and the answer is that the earth should tremble at the presence of the Lord, who turns the rock into a pool.`,[[`The sea and the mountains`,`Respond as living creatures to the presence of God.`],[`The Lord`,`Transforms Israel into His sanctuary and dominion, and brings water from a rock.`]],`The whole creation moves when God acts for His people.`],
[`"Not to us, Lord, not to us but to your name be the glory." The nations ask, "Where is their God?" The psalm answers that God is in heaven and does as He pleases, while idols are lifeless and those who make them become like them. The people are called to trust the Lord, who is their help and shield.`,[[`Israel`,`Called to trust in the Lord alone.`],[`The idols`,`Made of silver and gold, but have mouths and cannot speak.`]],`Glory belongs to God alone, and idols have no power.`],
[`"I love the Lord, for he heard my voice." The psalmist was nearly overcome by the cords of death, but the Lord saved him. "Return to your rest, my soul, for the Lord has been good to you." He asks, "How can I repay the Lord?" and answers by raising the cup of salvation and fulfilling his vows. "Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his faithful servants."`,[[`The psalmist`,`Grateful for deliverance from near death.`],[`The Lord`,`Gracious, righteous and full of compassion, who cares for the simple.`]],`Gratitude for rescue leads to a lifetime of public thanks.`],
[`The shortest chapter in the Bible. "Praise the Lord, all you nations; extol him, all you peoples." His love for us is great, and the faithfulness of the Lord endures for ever. Praise the Lord.`,[[`The nations`,`Called, with all peoples, to join in praise.`],[`The Lord`,`Great in love and eternal in faithfulness.`]],`Though short, it states the mission of the Church: the nations are invited to worship.`],
[`"Give thanks to the Lord, for he is good; his love endures for ever." The psalmist was surrounded by nations, but in the name of the Lord he cut them off. "The Lord is my strength and my defence; he has become my salvation." He enters the gates of righteousness, and says, "The stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone." The day of the Lord is to be celebrated with rejoicing.`,[[`The psalmist`,`A leader who was pressed hard and fell, but the Lord helped him.`],[`The rejected stone`,`Becomes the cornerstone, a verse quoted of Christ in the New Testament.`]],`It is the last of the Hallel psalms, and was sung at Passover; Jesus entered Jerusalem to its words.`],
[`The longest chapter of the Bible, an acrostic with twenty-two sections of eight verses, each beginning with a letter of the Hebrew alphabet. It celebrates God's word, calling it law, statutes, precepts, decrees, commands, ordinances and promises. "Your word is a lamp for my feet, a light on my path." The psalmist loves, meditates on and obeys it, and prays for help to stay faithful even when suffering.`,[[`The psalmist`,`Loves God's word more than gold, and clings to it in persecution.`],[`God's word`,`Gives life, wisdom, hope and guidance, and is eternal.`]],`Scripture is how God teaches, corrects and sustains His people every day.`],
[`The first of the Songs of Ascents, sung by pilgrims going up to Jerusalem. The psalmist is in distress and asks the Lord to rescue him from lying lips and deceitful tongues. He lives among those who hate peace, and says, "I am for peace; but when I speak, they are for war."`,[[`The psalmist`,`A pilgrim far from home among hostile people.`],[`The liars`,`Are warned of the sharp arrows of a warrior.`]],`Pilgrims begin their journey by asking God to protect them from deceit.`],
[`"I lift up my eyes to the mountains: where does my help come from? My help comes from the Lord, the Maker of heaven and earth." The Lord watches over the traveller and will not let his foot slip. He never slumbers or sleeps. The Lord is a shade at the right hand, and will keep His people from all harm, now and for ever.`,[[`The pilgrim`,`Looks to the hills of Jerusalem, and past them to the Lord who made them.`],[`The Lord`,`Keeper and protector, awake day and night.`]],`A pilgrim's confidence is not in the road but in the God who watches over it.`],
[`"I rejoiced with those who said to me, 'Let us go to the house of the Lord.'" Pilgrims stand within the gates of Jerusalem, a city that is closely compacted together, where the tribes go up to give thanks. David prays for the peace of Jerusalem, for the sake of his family and friends and the house of the Lord.`,[[`The pilgrims`,`Glad to arrive in the city of worship.`],[`David`,`Prays for peace and prosperity for the city and its people.`]],`Gathering to worship is a joy, and God's people are called to seek the good of His city.`],
[`"I lift up my eyes to you, to you who sit enthroned in heaven." Like servants watching their master's hand, the people look to the Lord until He shows mercy. They ask for mercy because they have endured much contempt and scorn from the proud.`,[[`The people`,`Humble servants who look to God and wait.`],[`The proud`,`Mock and treat God's people with contempt.`]],`When ridiculed, the right response is to keep our eyes on God and wait for His mercy.`],
[`"If the Lord had not been on our side, ... they would have swallowed us alive." David looks back at a flood of enemies that would have swept Israel away. "We have escaped like a bird from the fowler's snare; the snare has been broken, and we have escaped. Our help is in the name of the Lord, the Maker of heaven and earth."`,[[`David`,`Celebrates a narrow escape and attributes it to God.`],[`The Lord`,`Is on His people's side and breaks the trap.`]],`Our survival is not our own doing, but the Lord's.`],
[`Those who trust in the Lord are like Mount Zion, which cannot be shaken but endures for ever. As the mountains surround Jerusalem, so the Lord surrounds His people. The wicked will not rule the land permanently, lest the righteous use their hands to do evil. The psalm ends with a prayer for peace on Israel.`,[[`The people who trust`,`Steady and secure, because of what surrounds them.`],[`The Lord`,`Surrounds His people now and for ever, and does good to the upright.`]],`Security comes from the Lord's presence around us, not our circumstances.`],
[`"When the Lord restored the fortunes of Zion, we were like those who dreamed." Their mouths were filled with laughter and their tongues with songs of joy. Now they ask to be restored again, like streams in the Negev. "Those who sow with tears will reap with songs of joy."`,[[`The returned exiles`,`Remember joy at the first homecoming and pray for more.`],[`The sowers`,`Go out weeping, carrying seed, and come back with sheaves.`]],`Faithful work in hard times will end in joy.`],
[`"Unless the Lord builds the house, the builders labour in vain." Early rising and late working are in vain if the Lord is not in it, since He grants sleep to those He loves. Children are a heritage from the Lord, like arrows in the hands of a warrior. Blessed is the man whose quiver is full.`,[[`Solomon`,`Teaches that all work, security and family depend on God.`],[`Children`,`Described as a reward and gift from the Lord.`]],`Success does not come from anxious striving, but from depending on God.`],
[`"Blessed are all who fear the Lord, who walk in obedience to him." They will eat the fruit of their labour. The wife is like a fruitful vine, and the children are like olive shoots around the table. The psalm ends with a blessing from Zion that the person would see the prosperity of Jerusalem and live to see their children's children.`,[[`The one who fears the Lord`,`Is blessed in work and home.`],[`The family`,`Gathered around the table as a sign of God's goodness.`]],`The blessing of God shapes ordinary life: work, marriage and children.`],
[`"They have greatly oppressed me from my youth," Israel says, "but they have not gained the victory over me." Plowmen have ploughed upon my back, but the Lord is righteous and has cut me free from the cords of the wicked. Those who hate Zion are like grass on the roof that withers before it can grow.`,[[`Israel`,`Has suffered for many years, but has been kept.`],[`The enemies of Zion`,`Like grass on a roof, they wither and no one blesses them.`]],`The people of God have been persecuted but never destroyed.`],
[`"Out of the depths I cry to you, Lord." If the Lord kept a record of sins, no one could stand, but with Him there is forgiveness, so that He may be feared. The psalmist waits for the Lord more than watchmen wait for the morning, and calls on Israel to put their hope in the Lord, for with Him is unfailing love and full redemption.`,[[`The psalmist`,`In deep trouble and aware of his sin, he waits with hope.`],[`The Lord`,`Forgives, and redeems Israel from all their sins.`]],`This penitential psalm shows that forgiveness is the ground of hope.`],
[`David says his heart is not proud, and his eyes are not haughty. He does not concern himself with great matters or things too wonderful for him. "I have calmed and quieted myself, like a weaned child with its mother." He tells Israel to put their hope in the Lord now and for evermore.`,[[`David`,`Quiet, humble and content, like a child resting with its mother.`],[`Israel`,`Invited to hope in the Lord rather than striving.`]],`Humility finds peace in trusting God instead of understanding everything.`],
[`The psalmist remembers David's vow not to rest until he finds a place for the Lord, and the bringing of the ark to Zion. He prays that God would remember David and keep His oath that David's descendants would sit on the throne for ever. The Lord has chosen Zion, and says, "This is my resting place for ever."`,[[`David`,`Zealous to find a dwelling place for God.`],[`The Lord`,`Swore to David that his line would endure if they kept His covenant, and that He would clothe the priests with salvation.`]],`God's promises to David and Zion look forward to the Messiah.`],
[`"How good and pleasant it is when God's people live together in unity!" It is like precious oil poured on the head of Aaron, running down his beard and robes, and like the dew of Hermon falling on Mount Zion. There the Lord bestows His blessing, even life for evermore.`,[[`David`,`Praises the beauty of harmony among God's people.`],[`The Lord`,`Commands His blessing where His people live in unity.`]],`Unity is a gift that refreshes and brings blessing.`],
[`The last of the Songs of Ascents. The servants of the Lord who minister by night in the house of the Lord are told to lift up their hands in the sanctuary and praise Him. They in turn receive a blessing: "May the Lord bless you from Zion, he who is the Maker of heaven and earth."`,[[`The night watchmen`,`Priests and Levites who serve while others sleep.`],[`The Lord`,`Blessed in worship and the source of blessing from Zion.`]],`The pilgrims' journey ends with praise, and with a blessing sent back out.`],
[`"Praise the name of the Lord." The Lord has chosen Jacob for Himself and is greater than all gods. He does whatever pleases Him, makes clouds rise, sends lightning with rain, and struck down the firstborn of Egypt. The idols of the nations are silver and gold, made by human hands, and those who trust them become like them.`,[[`The servants of the Lord`,`Called to praise the God who is good.`],[`The idols`,`Have mouths but cannot speak, and eyes but cannot see.`]],`The living God acts in history and creation, while idols are lifeless.`],
[`Every verse ends with the refrain, "His love endures for ever." The psalm thanks God as the Lord of lords, the creator of the heavens, earth, sun, moon and stars, the one who struck the firstborn of Egypt and brought Israel out, divided the sea, and gave the land as an inheritance. He remembered us in our low estate.`,[[`The congregation`,`Respond to each statement with the same refrain.`],[`The Lord`,`Whose love is seen in creation, redemption and daily food.`]],`God's steady love is the thread that runs through creation and the history of His people.`],
[`"By the rivers of Babylon we sat and wept when we remembered Zion." The exiles hung their harps on the poplars, and their captors asked for songs of joy. "How can we sing the songs of the Lord while in a foreign land?" They vow never to forget Jerusalem, and the psalm ends with a fierce prayer against Babylon and Edom.`,[[`The exiles`,`Heartbroken and unwilling to pretend that all is well.`],[`The captors`,`Mock their grief and ask for entertainment.`]],`Grief and anger over injustice can be brought honestly to God.`],
[`David thanks the Lord with all his heart and sings before the gods. He praises God's love and faithfulness, because God has exalted His name and His word above all things. When he called, God answered. Kings of the earth will praise the Lord, for though He is on high, He looks kindly on the lowly. "The Lord will vindicate me; your love, Lord, endures for ever."`,[[`David`,`Thankful and confident that God will fulfil His purpose.`],[`The Lord`,`High and holy, and yet kind to the humble.`]],`God hears prayer and fulfils His purposes in our lives.`],
[`"You have searched me, Lord, and you know me." God knows when David sits and rises, perceives his thoughts, and is familiar with all his ways. There is nowhere to flee from His presence: whether in heaven, in the depths, or on the far side of the sea. God knit him together in his mother's womb. David asks God to search his heart and lead him in the way everlasting.`,[[`David`,`Amazed and humbled by being fully known.`],[`God`,`Everywhere present, all-knowing, and the maker of every person.`]],`To be fully known and fully loved by God is the foundation of life, and every person is wonderfully made.`],
[`David asks the Lord to rescue him from evil people who stir up war and whose tongues are like a snake's. He asks to be kept from the hands of the wicked and from violent people who have set a trap. He is sure that the Lord secures justice for the poor and the upright will live in His presence.`,[[`David`,`Faces slander and plots, and commits his case to the Lord.`],[`The violent`,`Their own evil will fall on them.`]],`God defends the cause of the poor and brings justice.`],
[`"May my prayer be set before you like incense; may the lifting up of my hands be like the evening sacrifice." David asks God to set a guard over his mouth and to keep his heart from what is evil. He would rather be rebuked by the righteous than honoured by the wicked. His eyes are fixed on the Lord, and he prays for protection from traps.`,[[`David`,`Asks for self-control and for the humility to accept correction.`],[`The righteous`,`Their rebuke is a kindness, like oil on the head.`]],`A prayer for the integrity of our words, desires and friends.`],
[`A prayer when David was in the cave. He cries aloud to the Lord and pours out his complaint, saying his spirit grows faint and no one cares for him. "You are my refuge, my portion in the land of the living." He asks to be set free from prison, so that he can praise the Lord's name.`,[[`David`,`Alone and trapped in a cave, but he knows to whom to turn.`],[`The Lord`,`Is a refuge when there is no one else.`]],`Even when abandoned, we can pour out our hearts to a God who listens.`],
[`David pleads with God to hear his prayer, and not to judge him, for no one living is righteous before Him. His enemies have crushed him, and his spirit grows faint. He remembers days long ago and stretches out his hands. "Let the morning bring me word of your unfailing love." He asks for guidance and for God's good Spirit to lead him.`,[[`David`,`Weary and in need of mercy, relying on God's righteousness and not his own.`],[`The Lord`,`Teaches His servant to do His will and leads him on level ground.`]],`This penitential psalm acknowledges that we are not righteous and depend entirely on grace.`],
[`"Praise be to the Lord my Rock, who trains my hands for war." David marvels that God cares for mortals who are like a breath. He asks God to come down and rescue him, and promises a new song. The psalm ends with a prayer for a thriving nation in which the people are secure and "blessed are the people whose God is the Lord."`,[[`David`,`A warrior-king who knows his strength comes from God.`],[`The people`,`Described as blessed, with sons and daughters, full barns and peace in the streets.`]],`True national blessing comes from having the Lord as God.`],
[`David's psalm of praise, an acrostic: "I will exalt you, my God the King." Every generation will commend the Lord's works to the next. He is gracious and compassionate, slow to anger and rich in love. The Lord is good to all, and His compassion is over all He has made. "The Lord is near to all who call on him."`,[[`David`,`Offers daily praise and invites everyone to join.`],[`The Lord`,`King over all, faithful, upholding the fallen and satisfying every living thing.`]],`It is the only psalm called simply "A psalm of praise," and its theme is God's greatness and goodness.`],
[`The first of the five closing Hallelujah psalms. "Praise the Lord, my soul." The psalmist promises to praise God as long as he lives, and tells us not to put our trust in princes. The Lord is the Maker of heaven and earth, who upholds the oppressed, feeds the hungry, frees prisoners, opens the eyes of the blind and watches over the foreigner and the widow.`,[[`The psalmist`,`Chooses to put hope in God, not in human rulers.`],[`The Lord`,`Reigns for ever and cares for those who have no one else.`]],`Human help is temporary, while God is faithful for ever.`],
[`"How good it is to sing praises to our God." The Lord builds up Jerusalem, gathers the exiles, heals the brokenhearted and binds up their wounds. He counts the stars and calls them by name, sends rain, snow and frost, and gives His word to Jacob. He delights in those who fear Him and put their hope in His unfailing love.`,[[`The Lord`,`Great in power, and gentle toward the broken.`],[`Jerusalem`,`Is rebuilt and given peace and the finest wheat.`]],`The God who names the stars also heals the brokenhearted.`],
[`All creation is called to praise the Lord: angels, heavenly hosts, sun, moon, stars, sea creatures, mountains, trees, animals, kings, young and old. At His command they were created. He has raised up a horn for His people, and all will praise Him.`,[[`Creation`,`Summoned from the highest heavens to the depths of the ocean to praise the Lord.`],[`The people of Israel`,`Close to His heart, with a horn of strength lifted up.`]],`Everything that exists is called to give glory to its Creator.`],
[`"Sing to the Lord a new song." Israel is to rejoice in its Maker and dance and make music. The Lord takes delight in His people and crowns the humble with victory. The faithful are to praise with high praise in their mouths and a double-edged sword in their hands to carry out God's judgement on the nations.`,[[`The faithful`,`Celebrate, and share in what the Lord does to judge evil.`],[`The Lord`,`Delights in His people and honours the humble.`]],`Worship and the work of justice go together under God's rule.`],
[`The final psalm is a call to praise the Lord everywhere and with everything: in His sanctuary, in His mighty heavens, for His acts of power and His surpassing greatness. Trumpet, harp, lyre, tambourine, strings, flute and cymbals are all invited. "Let everything that has breath praise the Lord."`,[[`Every living thing`,`Called to respond with joy, loud and clear.`],[`The Lord`,`Praised for who He is and what He has done.`]],`The whole book of Psalms ends with the one command that sums up its purpose: praise.`]
];
(window.CHAPWHY=window.CHAPWHY||{})[18]={"1":"The Psalms begin with a choice between two paths: delight in God's word brings lasting fruit, and ignoring him leaves nothing to stand on.","2":"God's chosen King rules whatever the nations plot, and the New Testament applies this to Jesus, the Son. The wise take refuge in him.","3":"You can sleep in peace even with enemies all around, because the Lord is a shield.","4":"Peace at night comes from trusting God's care, not from calm circumstances.","5":"Begin the day by bringing your needs to God and then waiting for his answer.","6":"Honest weakness and tears are welcome in prayer, because God hears weeping.","7":"When falsely accused, you can hand the case over to the righteous Judge.","8":"We are small beside the heavens, yet God crowns humans with honour. Hebrews 2 sees this fulfilled in Jesus.","9":"Remembering what God has done builds trust that he will not forget the oppressed.","10":"It is honest to ask why God seems far away, and the psalm ends trusting that he hears the afflicted.","11":"When everything seems to be collapsing, the answer is not to flee but to remember that God still reigns.","12":"When lies and injustice multiply, God's words stay pure and he promises to rise up for the poor.","13":"This is a pattern for lament: ask \"how long?\" honestly, then end in trust and song.","14":"Life without God leads to corruption, and Paul quotes this psalm to show that everyone needs God's rescue.","15":"Those who live close to God are honest, faithful to their word and generous with others.","16":"God himself is the believer's treasure, and death does not have the last word. Peter points to the resurrection in Acts 2.","17":"Those God guards as the apple of his eye find their deepest satisfaction in seeing him.","18":"God is the Rock who rescues, and David's song of deliverance appears again in 2 Samuel 22.","19":"Creation and Scripture both reveal God, and the psalm ends by asking that our words and hearts please him.","20":"Real security is trusting the name of the Lord, not chariots and horses.","21":"God answers prayer, and the king's stability rests on God's love, pointing ahead to the ultimate King.","22":"Jesus prayed the opening words on the cross, and the psalm shows suffering leading to praise among all nations.","23":"God cares for his people like a shepherd, in times of plenty and in the darkest valley.","24":"The earth belongs to God, and only clean hands and a pure heart may approach him, while the King of glory opens the way.","25":"Wait on God for guidance and forgiveness, even when you feel lonely and troubled.","26":"Integrity means being willing to be examined by God, and it is lived out in worship.","27":"Seeking God's presence is the one thing that drives out fear.","28":"Prayer moves from desperate cry to praise as God answers, and ends by asking him to shepherd his people.","29":"God's power in the storm calls us to worship, and he gives strength and peace to his people.","30":"Sorrow is not permanent, because God turns mourning into dancing."};